Perfect — your `index.ts` shows exactly how the front end fetches the HTML! Let’s confirm what’s happening and fix the URL to point to your **running server extension** instead of a bundled static file.  

---

### ✅ **Right now**
```ts
const htmlUrl = new URL('../static/about.html', import.meta.url).toString();
```
This tries to load `about.html` from the *bundled* static folder **inside the labextension**, not from the live server extension. That’s why you see stale or missing content if the HTML isn’t packed there.

---

### ✅ **What you want**
Since your server extension serves `/bearborg/index.html`, you should:
```ts
const htmlUrl = '/bearborg/index.html';
```

No need for `new URL(...)` or `import.meta.url`. Just use the direct route served by your server extension. JupyterLab’s front end will call your backend on the same port, so it works seamlessly.

---

### ✅ **Updated snippet**
Here’s the fix in your `newWidget` function:
```ts
const newWidget = () => {
  const content = new Widget();
  const htmlUrl = '/bearborg/index.html';  // Serve from server extension

  fetch(htmlUrl)
    .then(response => response.text())
    .then(html => {
      content.node.innerHTML = html;
    });

  content.node.style.overflow = 'auto';
  content.node.style.height = '100%';
  content.addClass('jp-About-content');

  const widget = new MainAreaWidget({ content });
  widget.id = 'bearborg-help';
  widget.title.label = 'About BearBorg';
  widget.title.closable = true;

  return widget;
};
```

---

### ✅ **Why this works**
- It uses the server extension route: `/bearborg/index.html`  
- No more stale or missing static files.  
- No need to rebuild your labextension every time you update the HTML!  

When you restart JupyterLab, the front end calls your server extension, which always serves the latest file from disk.

---

