But there’s a **tiny bug** in your iframe HTML: the `<iframe>` tag is self-closing, so the inner `</iframe>` is missing.  

Here’s the **corrected version** of your snippet, ready to paste:

```ts
// Define a widget creator function
const newWidget = () => {
  const content = new Widget();
  const htmlUrl = '/bearborg/';  // ✅ Root URL — let the server serve index.html

  content.node.innerHTML = `
    <iframe
      src="${htmlUrl}"
      style="width: 100%; height: 100%; border: none;"
    ></iframe>
  `;

  content.node.style.overflow = 'auto';
  content.node.style.height = '100%';

  // Optional: custom styling
  content.addClass('jp-About-content');

  const widget = new MainAreaWidget({ content });
  widget.id = 'bearborg-help';
  widget.title.label = 'About BearBorg';
  widget.title.closable = true;

  return widget;
};
```

**✅ Key fix:**  
- The `<iframe>` must be `<iframe>...</iframe>` — you can’t self-close it like `<iframe />`.

**✅ Also:**  
- Using `/bearborg/` instead of `/bearborg/index.html` is best — your server’s index handler covers it.
- Your iframe has `width: 100%` and `height: 100%` so it’ll fill the JupyterLab tab properly.

